<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<resources>
    <color name="widget_background">#FBF7F1</color>
    <color name="widget_accent">#2E7D32</color>
    <color name="widget_text_primary">#1C1B1F</color>
    <color name="widget_text_secondary">#6E6A63</color>
    <!--
        The pre-Android-12 `previewImage` vector's today fill. The live render fills today's cell with
        `widget_selected_day` (today is the default selection), and this is the same value for the one
        preview tier that cannot reference the live token. Kept as its own name rather than aliased to
        or deleted in favour of `widget_selected_day`, because removing a resource a consumer may
        reference is a breaking change.
    -->
    <color name="widget_today_background">#2E7D32</color>
    <color name="widget_on_today">#FFFFFF</color>
    <color name="widget_weekend_text">#B3261E</color>

    <!--
        The three dimmed day figures (FD-07).

        These used to be `primaryText.copy(alpha = …)` computed in Kotlin, which had two costs: the
        alpha could not survive a night-mode switch (see below), and a colour could not be asserted.

        **They are now real resources, which means they are resolved by the launcher against its own
        configuration at bind time.** Before this they were literals baked into the composition, so a
        night-mode switch invalidated the whole `RemoteViews` and rebuilt it — the "restart" the report
        describes. There is a night variant of each.

        Each is `widget_text_primary` composited over `widget_background` at the alpha its name
        records, so they can be recomputed by hand if either of those two changes:

            day_out_of_month   primary at 38%   (the dimmed Hijri figure of a neighbouring month)
            day_gregorian_sub  primary at 60%   (the Gregorian day under an in-month Hijri figure)
            day_out_faint      primary at 24%   (the Gregorian day of a neighbouring month)

        A test asserts every colour resolves to a *different* value under `values-night`; it cannot
        assert the value itself, since that would only prove the XML says what the XML says.
    -->
    <color name="widget_day_out_of_month">#A6A3A1</color>
    <color name="widget_day_gregorian_sub">#757373</color>
    <color name="widget_day_out_faint">#C5C2BF</color>
    <color name="widget_arrow_dimmed">#8F8B85</color>
    <!--
        The cell divider (FD-04). The active-colour hairline: the palette accent at 40% over
        `widget_background`, so the grid reads as the calendar's own grid rather than as a grey
        spreadsheet rule. Still one hairline per row/column rather than a border per cell — the grid
        is 42 cells and a full border on each would cost 42 more views on a `previewLayout`.
    -->
    <color name="widget_cell_border">#662E7D32</color>
    <!--
        The fill of the chosen day (FD-09, redesigned). A full accent **block**, where this used to be
        a corner ring: a ring could not carry the accent without an alpha (which a `ColorProvider`
        cannot express) and read as decoration laid over the grid rather than as the chosen day. Today
        is the default selection, so an untouched widget fills today's cell with this and the block
        moves to whatever the user taps; a cell that is both today and selected shows only this.
    -->
    <color name="widget_selected_day">#2E7D32</color>
    <!--
        The observance fill (FD-08). A **container**, not the accent: `widget_selected_day` is the
        selection and `widget_accent` marks today's figure, so reusing either would make Ashura read as
        a chosen day. This is the accent at 14% over `widget_background` (#FBF7F1) — visible as a wash,
        still letting the day figure carry it.
    -->
    <color name="widget_event_day_background">#E4EEE2</color>
    <!-- `widget_text_primary` over the fill above: an observance day must not dim its own figure. -->
    <color name="widget_on_event_day">#1C1B1F</color>
</resources>